  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Ranjeet Agarwal Alias Ranjit Alias ... vs The State Of Jharkhand on 31 March, 2015 Author: Prashant Kumar Bench: Prashant Kumar In The High Court Of Jharkhand, Ranchi Cr. M.P. No. 2497 of 2014 Ranjeet Agawal @ Ranjit @ Ramesh son of Ramjash Agarwal, resident of Rani Sati Market, Jharia, P.O. & P.S. Jharia, District Dhanbad ... ... ... Petitioner Versus The State of Jharkhand ... ... ... Opp. Party Coram: The Hon'Ble Mr. Justice Prashant Kumar ............ For the Petitioner : Mrs. Jasvinder Mazumdar, Advocate For the State : Mr. H.K. Shikarwar, A.P.P. 2/31.03.2015",
    "facts": ", whereby and whereunder, learned court below took cognizance against the petitioner under Sections 414/273 of the IPC, 30 (2) of Food Safety Act 2006 & 2,3,4 of the Food Safety Prohibition and Restriction Act 2011. It is submitted that the Magistrate has no power to take cognizance of the offence, as under the Food Safety and Standards Act 2006, all the cases are triable by the Special Court. Accordingly, order taking cognizance is bad.",
    "arguments": "On the other hand, Sri H.K. Shikarwar, Additional PP submits that as per Section 74 of the aforesaid Act, it is not necessary for the State Government to constitute a Special Court, rather, in some particular case, the State Government has power to constitute the same. He further submits that as per Section 73 of the above Act, the Magistrate has power to try the case.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "On the other hand, Sri H.K. Shikarwar, Additional PP submits that as per Section 74 of the aforesaid Act, it is not necessary for the State Government to constitute a Special Court, rather, in some particular case, the State Government has power to constitute the same. He further submits that as per Section 73 of the above Act, the Magistrate has power to try the case.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
